id: k8s.stateful-set
kind: kubernetes
title: StatefulSet
aliases: statefulset, stateful workload
tags: workloads, state, storage
questions:
  - when do you use a StatefulSet instead of a Deployment?
invariant: Each replica keeps a stable ordinal identity and its own PersistentVolumeClaim across restarts.
answer_30s: StatefulSets give each replica a stable name (app-0, app-1), stable DNS and its own PVC; starting, stopping and scaling all happen in ordinal order.
answer_2m: |
  Identity is the point: Pod db-0 keeps its name, hostname and volume across
  rescheduling, so cluster membership and replication topology are recomputable
  from names instead of being random. Ordering is a consequence of that
  identity model, not the goal: with the default OrderedReady policy the
  controller waits for pod N to be Ready before creating pod N+1. Use it when a
  process needs durable identity -- databases, quorum systems, brokers -- not
  merely because it writes to disk.
when_to_use:
  - databases and quorum systems
  - per-replica durable identity or ordered start
avoid_when:
  - replicas are interchangeable (a Deployment is cheaper and scales fast)
commands:
  - kubectl get statefulset -o wide => desired vs ready replicas and current revision
  - kubectl get pvc => which claims exist per ordinal
  - kubectl describe statefulset db => why replica 1 has not been created yet
failure_modes:
  - deleting the StatefulSet leaves orphan PVCs behind (by design)
  - assuming a parallel rollout; OrderedReady is serial and slow
  - scaling down keeps the volume, so a later scale up reuses old data
follow_ups:
  - how does service discovery work for a StatefulSet?
related:
  - k8s.headless-service
  - k8s.pvc
  - k8s.deployment
  - recipe.stateful-workload
sources:
  - https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/controllers/statefulset/
